{
  "_about": "Wars fought away from the main fronts, each to its own plan. A theatre is a list of regions. A computer-run nation with a plan here runs every corps it has standing in those regions by that plan, and the rest of its army by its main plan in plan-historical.json: neither takes orders meant for the other, and battles on a theatre's ground roll their own dice. Phases read as they do there (from, to, posture, enemies, declare, ifAtWar, objectives, targets, siege, release, landings, minOdds, oddsFor, staging, concentrate, abandon). Here several phases may cover the same months: 'ifLost' makes a phase the plan only while the enemy holds every place it names, and the first phase in the list whose months and conditions fit is the one followed. 'bases' names the port each army falls back on when its plan gives ground up; a phase can name another place for the months it runs with 'base'. The force a phase keeps at a place with 'staging' holds it and joins no attack elsewhere: an army overseas has no reserves to spare. A landing can take other corps on the same quay in the same lift ('with'), and tries each place on its 'to' list in turn, passing over a shore it cannot storm at its 'minOdds'. 'quiet' means a defence that makes no local counter-attacks because the plan is saving the army for a later stroke. 'withdraw' is for an expedition on an ally's ground: once the enemy holds any place in 'when', its corps make for a port and are taken off. A theatre's 'havens' are where troops lifted off its coasts are taken, the first still in friendly hands with room, rather than all the way home; troops already in a haven stay there. A theatre can list 'works' dug by the calendar, as desert.json does: [month, place, level, who must hold it, the news]. With no phase for the month the army stands on the defensive. As everywhere, the plan only sets intentions: the ground still has to be won.",
